// The physical robots of the stack over MCP: the server built into peppy
// serves the robot_control:v1 exposure from the MCP hub on one endpoint,
// http://127.0.0.1:8900/robot_control/v1/mcp, where every call names its
// robot, reading wall time. A server reads one clock, so this is the server
// the physical robots enroll into; robot_control_sim.json5 is the twin on
// the simulation's clock the simulated robots enroll into. The document is
// a per-robot surface, so each of its targets is a set the robots fill:
// this instance takes no links, and a robot's own fragment adds its
// instances to the targets with `add_links` on robot_control_inst: every
// robot beside the server is listed with its identity, limb state and
// collision readout, and with its brain and recorder whenever they run; its
// `mcp_commander` option adds the moves, and its rig adds the cameras under
// that option. A join adds its robot's instances while the server runs and a
// removal takes them out, so the endpoint and its URL are the same for one
// robot or ten and before and after any join.
//
// The fleet deploys this option with `--with robot_control`.
{
  peppy_schema: "launcher_fragment/v1",

  deployments: [
    {
      source: { exposures: ["robot_control:v1"] },
      instances: [
        {
          instance_id: "robot_control_inst",
          // The derived node's only parameter: the port the server prefers
          // on 127.0.0.1. One server serves the fleet, so the port is the
          // stack's, beside the simulated world's endpoint on 8902.
          arguments: { port: 8900 },
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
}
